WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.950 --> 00:00:10.390
Math is sometimes a real tease it seduces us with the beauty off reasoning geometrically in two and three dimensions

00:00:10.850 --> 00:00:17.950
Where there's this really nice back-and-forth between pairs or triplets of numbers and spatial stuff that our visual Cortex is good at processing

00:00:18.710 --> 00:00:22.660
for example if you think about a circle with radius one centered at the origin

00:00:23.390 --> 00:00:30.400
You are in effect conceptualizing every possible pair of numbers x and y that satisfy a certain numerical

00:00:30.859 --> 00:00:34.209
property that x² + y² is 1 and

00:00:34.699 --> 00:00:39.099
The usefulness here is that a lot of facts that look opaque in a purely

00:00:39.679 --> 00:00:43.269
analytic context become quite clear geometrically and vice-versa

00:00:43.999 --> 00:00:48.638
Honestly this channel has been the direct beneficiary of this back and forth

00:00:49.069 --> 00:00:54.638
Since it offers such a rich library of that special category of cleverness that involves connecting two

00:00:54.949 --> 00:01:01.629
Seemingly disparate ideas, and I don't just mean the general back and forth between pairs or triplets of numbers and spatial reasoning

00:01:01.629 --> 00:01:05.979
I mean this specific one between sums of squares and circles and spheres

00:01:06.650 --> 00:01:08.530
It's at the heart of the video I made showing

00:01:08.530 --> 00:01:15.699
how pi is connected to number theory in primes and the one showing how to visualize all possible pythagorean triples.

00:01:15.950 --> 00:01:19.930
It also underlies the video on the Borsuk-Ulam theorem being used to solve

00:01:19.940 --> 00:01:24.789
What was basically a counting puzzle by using topological facts about spheres?

00:01:25.250 --> 00:01:31.000
There is no doubt that the ability to frame analytic facts geometrically is very useful for math

00:01:31.310 --> 00:01:38.140
But it's all a tease - because when you start asking questions about quadruplets or quintuplets or 100 tuples of numbers

00:01:38.390 --> 00:01:41.890
It's frustrating. The constraints on our physical space

00:01:42.290 --> 00:01:47.440
Seem to have constrained our intuitions about geometry and we lose this back and forth

00:01:47.440 --> 00:01:53.379
I mean it is completely reasonable to imagine that there are problems out there that would have clever and

00:01:53.570 --> 00:02:01.000
Illuminating solutions if only we knew how to conceptualize, say, lists of 10 numbers as individual points in some space

00:02:01.729 --> 00:02:05.109
for mathematicians or computer scientists or physicists

00:02:05.510 --> 00:02:11.799
problems that are framed in terms of lists of numbers - lists of more than three numbers - are a regular part of the job.

00:02:12.109 --> 00:02:18.879
And the standard approach to actually doing math in higher dimensions is to use two and three dimensions for analogy

00:02:19.000 --> 00:02:22.580
but to fundamentally reason about things just analytically.

00:02:22.880 --> 00:02:28.420
Somewhat analogous to a pilot relying primarily on instruments and not sight while flying through the clouds

00:02:28.940 --> 00:02:35.980
now what I want to offer here is a hybrid between the purely geometric and the purely analytic views. A method for making the

00:02:36.140 --> 00:02:41.740
analytic reasoning a little more visual in a way that generalizes to arbitrarily high dimensions and

00:02:41.930 --> 00:02:44.200
to drive home the value of a tactic like this

00:02:44.200 --> 00:02:46.450
I want to share with you a very famous example

00:02:46.910 --> 00:02:49.270
where analogies with two and three dimensions

00:02:49.580 --> 00:02:55.560
cannot help, because of something extremely counterintuitive that only happens in higher dimensions.

00:02:55.720 --> 00:03:00.680
The hope though, is that what I show you here helps to make that phenomenon more intuitive

00:03:01.640 --> 00:03:05.100
The focus throughout will be on higher-dimensional spheres.

00:03:05.400 --> 00:03:08.020
For example, when we talk about a four-dimensional sphere

00:03:08.200 --> 00:03:10.720
Say, with radius 1, centered at the origin.

00:03:11.020 --> 00:03:15.420
What that actually is is the set of all quadruplets of numbers

00:03:15.480 --> 00:03:19.340
x, y, z, w, where the sum of the squares of these numbers is 1.

00:03:19.970 --> 00:03:22.779
What I have pictured here now is multiple

00:03:23.360 --> 00:03:25.809
three-dimensional slices of a 4D sphere

00:03:26.390 --> 00:03:28.390
projected back into three dimensions

00:03:28.840 --> 00:03:30.580
but it's confusing and

00:03:30.600 --> 00:03:32.600
even if you do wrap your head around it,

00:03:32.600 --> 00:03:35.020
it just pushes the question back, to how you would think

00:03:35.040 --> 00:03:38.020
about a 5- or a 6- or a 7-dimensional sphere

00:03:38.020 --> 00:03:42.400
and more importantly squinting your eyes to understand a projection like this

00:03:42.440 --> 00:03:47.680
is not very reflective of what doing math with a 4D sphere actually entails.

00:03:48.830 --> 00:03:54.339
Instead, the basic idea here will be to get very literal about it and to think about four separate numbers

00:03:54.340 --> 00:04:00.260
I like to picture for vertical number lines with sliders to represent each number.

00:04:00.340 --> 00:04:06.040
Each configuration of these sliders is a point in 4D space: A quadruplet of numbers.

00:04:06.160 --> 00:04:10.700
And what it means to be on a 4D unit sphere centered at the origin,

00:04:10.760 --> 00:04:14.200
is that the sum of the squares of these four values is 1.

00:04:16.480 --> 00:04:23.520
Our goal is to understand which movements of these sliders correspond to movements on the sphere.

00:04:25.200 --> 00:04:30.200
To do that, it helps if we knock things down to two dimensions where we can actually see the circle.

00:04:30.300 --> 00:04:31.200
So ask yourself,

00:04:31.380 --> 00:04:36.520
What's a nice way to think about this relation that x²+y² is 1?

00:04:37.600 --> 00:04:42.540
Well, I like to think of the value of x² as the real estate belonging to x,

00:04:42.540 --> 00:04:46.600
and likewise the value of y² is the real estate belonging to y,

00:04:46.600 --> 00:04:50.540
and that they have a total of 1 unit of real estate to share between them.

00:04:51.130 --> 00:04:53.429
So moving around on the circle

00:04:53.980 --> 00:04:57.959
Corresponds to a constant exchange of real estate between the two variables

00:04:59.169 --> 00:05:02.339
Part of the reason I choose this term is that it lets us make a very useful

00:05:02.440 --> 00:05:07.679
Analogy: That real estate is cheap near zero, and more expensive away from zero.

00:05:08.260 --> 00:05:13.109
To see this, consider starting off in a position where x=1 and y is zero.

00:05:13.330 --> 00:05:15.960
Meaning x has all of the real estate to itself

00:05:16.240 --> 00:05:20.609
which in our usual geometric picture means we're on the right most point of the circle.

00:05:20.950 --> 00:05:25.559
If you move x down just a bit to 0.9 the value of x²

00:05:25.930 --> 00:05:32.489
changes to 0.81, so it has in effect given up 0.19 units of real estate

00:05:32.950 --> 00:05:35.819
But for y² to increase by that same amount

00:05:36.400 --> 00:05:43.019
Y has to move an entire 0.44 units away from zero more than four times the amount that x moved

00:05:43.479 --> 00:05:47.519
in other words, X changed a little to give up expensive real estate,

00:05:47.590 --> 00:05:51.989
So that y could move a lot and gain the same value of cheap real estate.

00:05:52.630 --> 00:05:57.779
In terms of the usual circle drawing this corresponds to the steep slope near the right side.

00:05:58.000 --> 00:06:01.440
A small nudge in x allows for a very big change to y

00:06:02.530 --> 00:06:06.089
Moving forward, let's add some tick marks to these lines to indicate

00:06:06.780 --> 00:06:09.860
what 0.05 units of real estate looks like at each point

00:06:10.120 --> 00:06:16.740
that is how much would x have to change so that the value of x² changes by 0.05

00:06:17.220 --> 00:06:22.120
As you walk around the circle the trade-off in value between x² and y²

00:06:22.120 --> 00:06:27.780
gives this piston looking dance motion, where the sliders are moving more slowly away from 0

00:06:28.240 --> 00:06:31.400
Because real estate is more expensive in those regions.

00:06:31.480 --> 00:06:33.800
There are just more tick marks to cover per unit distance

00:06:34.940 --> 00:06:42.680
Also, a nice side effect of the term real estate is that it aligns naturally with the fact that it comes in units of distance squared.

00:06:42.740 --> 00:06:49.140
So the square root of the total real estate among all coordinates gives us the distance from the origin.

00:06:50.240 --> 00:06:56.200
For a unit sphere in 3 dimensions the set of all triplets x,y,z, where the sum of their squares is 1

00:06:56.400 --> 00:06:59.570
All we have to do is add a third slider for z

00:06:59.940 --> 00:07:04.300
But these three sliders still only have the one unit of real estate to share between them

00:07:04.760 --> 00:07:08.780
To get a feel for this imagine holding x in place at 0.5,

00:07:08.940 --> 00:07:12.700
where it occupies 0.25 units of real Estate

00:07:13.080 --> 00:07:15.740
What this means, is that y and z can move around

00:07:15.760 --> 00:07:18.420
in the same piston dance motion we saw before

00:07:18.440 --> 00:07:22.660
as they trade off the remaining 0.75 units of real Estate.

00:07:23.220 --> 00:07:26.220
In terms of our typical way of visualizing a sphere,

00:07:26.220 --> 00:07:31.320
this corresponds to slicing the sphere along the plane where x is 0.5

00:07:31.520 --> 00:07:36.460
and looking at the circle formed by all of the choices for Y and Z on that sphere.

00:07:37.470 --> 00:07:43.309
As you increase the value of x the amount of real estate left over for Y and Z is smaller

00:07:43.440 --> 00:07:48.499
And this more constrained piston dance is what it feels like for the circular slice to be smaller

00:07:49.290 --> 00:07:51.769
eventually once x reaches the value 1

00:07:51.900 --> 00:07:58.130
there's no real estate left over, so you reach this singularity point where y and z are both forced to be 0

00:07:59.250 --> 00:08:02.839
The feeling here is a bit like being a bug on the surface of the sphere

00:08:03.330 --> 00:08:06.679
You are unable to see the whole sphere all at once instead

00:08:07.110 --> 00:08:11.930
You're just sitting on a single point, and you have some sense for what local movements are allowed

00:08:15.850 --> 00:08:20.760
In 4 dimensions and higher we lose the crutch of the global view that a spatial visual offers

00:08:20.950 --> 00:08:24.870
But the fundamental rules of this real estate exchange remain the same

00:08:25.270 --> 00:08:29.190
If you fix one slider in place and watch the other three trade off

00:08:29.620 --> 00:08:35.819
This is basically what it means to take a slice of the 4D sphere to get a small 3D sphere in

00:08:35.920 --> 00:08:39.389
Much the same way that fixing one of the sliders for the three dimensional case

00:08:39.730 --> 00:08:43.170
Give us a circular slice when the remaining two were free to vary

00:08:44.800 --> 00:08:50.039
Now watching these sliders move about and thinking about the real estate exchange is pretty fun

00:08:50.350 --> 00:08:56.100
but it runs the risk of being aimless unless we have an actual high dimensional puzzle to sink our teeth into

00:08:56.710 --> 00:09:03.329
so let's set aside the sliders for just a moment and bring in a very classic example of something that seems reasonable and

00:09:03.520 --> 00:09:05.960
even dull in two and three dimensions

00:09:06.120 --> 00:09:09.360
But which is totally out of whack in higher dimensions.

00:09:10.660 --> 00:09:13.680
To start, take a 2x2 box centered at the origin

00:09:14.410 --> 00:09:20.490
Its corners around the vertices (1, 1), (1, -1),  (-1, 1) and (-1, -1).

00:09:21.310 --> 00:09:24.179
Draw four circles each with radius one centered

00:09:24.820 --> 00:09:29.460
At these four vertices so each one is tangent to two of its neighbors

00:09:30.120 --> 00:09:36.740
Now I want you to think of the circle centered at the origin which is just large enough to be touching those corner circles

00:09:36.940 --> 00:09:39.000
Tangent to each one of them

00:09:39.010 --> 00:09:45.780
What we want to do for this setup and for its analogies in higher dimensions is find the radius of that inner circle

00:09:47.560 --> 00:09:48.660
Here in two dimensions

00:09:48.660 --> 00:09:51.779
We can use the pythagorean theorem to see that the distance from the origin

00:09:52.240 --> 00:09:57.359
To the corner of the box is the square root of two which is around 1.414

00:09:57.910 --> 00:10:03.810
Then you can subtract off this portion here, the radius of the corner circle, which by definition is 1

00:10:04.210 --> 00:10:10.710
And that means the radius of the inner circle is square root of 2 minus 1 or about 0.414

00:10:11.110 --> 00:10:13.529
No surprises here, that seems pretty reasonable.

00:10:14.590 --> 00:10:17.500
Now do something analogous in three dimensions:

00:10:17.500 --> 00:10:19.530
draw a 2 x 2 x 2 cube.

00:10:19.720 --> 00:10:23.910
Whose corners have vertices (1, 1, 1), (1, 1, -1), on and on and on.

00:10:24.719 --> 00:10:30.529
and then we're gonna take eight different spheres each of which has a radius one and

00:10:30.929 --> 00:10:36.048
Center them on these vertices so that each one is tangent to three of its neighbors

00:10:36.779 --> 00:10:44.328
Now again think about the sphere centered at the origin which is just large enough to be barely touching those eight corner spheres

00:10:44.669 --> 00:10:49.908
As before, we can start by thinking about the distance from the origin to the corner of the box

00:10:50.069 --> 00:10:52.069
say the corner at (1, 1, 1)

00:10:53.249 --> 00:10:53.959
By the way

00:10:53.959 --> 00:10:58.638
I guess I still haven't yet explicitly said that the way distances work in higher dimensions is

00:10:58.979 --> 00:11:04.278
Always to add up the squares of the components in each direction and take the square root

00:11:04.679 --> 00:11:09.858
If you've never seen why this follows from the pythagorean theorem just in the two-dimensional case

00:11:10.019 --> 00:11:15.918
it's actually a really fun puzzle to think about and I've left the relevant image up on the screen for any of you who want

00:11:15.919 --> 00:11:17.849
want to pause and ponder on it.

00:11:17.849 --> 00:11:22.699
Anyway, in our case the distance between the origin and the corner (1, 1, 1) is the

00:11:22.919 --> 00:11:28.848
Square root of 1²+1²+1² or square root of 3, which is about 1.73

00:11:29.460 --> 00:11:32.980
So the radius of that inner sphere is going to be this quantity

00:11:33.480 --> 00:11:37.980
Minus the radius of a corner sphere, which by definition is 1.

00:11:38.060 --> 00:11:43.840
And again, 0.73 seems like a reasonable radius for that inner sphere.

00:11:43.840 --> 00:11:47.040
But what happens to that inner radius as you increase dimensions?

00:11:47.999 --> 00:11:51.680
Obviously the reason I bring this up. Is that something surprising will happen,

00:11:51.680 --> 00:11:53.780
and some of you might see where this is going.

00:11:53.880 --> 00:11:57.120
But actually don't want it to feel like a surprise -

00:11:57.120 --> 00:12:00.440
as fun as it is to wow people with counterintuitive facts in math

00:12:00.689 --> 00:12:03.738
The goal here is genuine understanding, not shock

00:12:04.109 --> 00:12:08.689
For higher dimensions we'll be using sliders to get a gut feel for what's going on

00:12:08.789 --> 00:12:14.329
But since it's kind of a different way of viewing things it helps to get a running start by looking back at how to analyze

00:12:14.329 --> 00:12:17.658
the two and three-dimensional cases in the context of sliders

00:12:18.720 --> 00:12:20.180
First things first,

00:12:20.180 --> 00:12:24.109
how do you think about a circle centered at a corner, like (1, -1)?

00:12:24.689 --> 00:12:27.348
Well previously for a circle centered at the origin

00:12:27.929 --> 00:12:34.009
the amount of real estate belonging to both x and y was dependent on their distance from the number 0

00:12:34.700 --> 00:12:38.030
And it's the same basic idea here as you move around the center

00:12:38.030 --> 00:12:42.980
It's just that the real estate might be dependent on the distance between each coordinate and some other number

00:12:43.230 --> 00:12:46.009
so for this circle centered at (1, -1)

00:12:46.380 --> 00:12:51.410
the amount of real estate belonging to x is the square of its distance from 1

00:12:52.080 --> 00:12:57.319
Likewise the real estate belonging to y is the square of its distance from negative 1

00:12:57.720 --> 00:13:02.209
Other than that the looking feel with this piston dance trade-off is completely the same

00:13:03.270 --> 00:13:07.729
for simplicity we'll only focus on one of these circles the one centered at (1, 1)

00:13:08.400 --> 00:13:09.360
Now ask yourself

00:13:09.360 --> 00:13:11.990
What does it mean to find a circle centered at the origin?

00:13:12.480 --> 00:13:16.700
Large enough to be tangent to this guy when we're thinking just in terms of sliders

00:13:20.010 --> 00:13:25.280
Well, notice how this point of tangency happens when the x and y coordinates are both the same

00:13:25.500 --> 00:13:30.530
Or phrased differently, at the point of this corner circle closest to the origin

00:13:31.080 --> 00:13:34.249
The real estate is shared evenly between x and y

00:13:35.010 --> 00:13:38.869
This will be important for later. So let's really dig in and think about why it's true

00:13:39.960 --> 00:13:42.200
Imagine perturbing that point slightly.

00:13:42.200 --> 00:13:47.119
Maybe moving x a little closer to 0 which means y would have to move a little away from 0

00:13:47.610 --> 00:13:51.260
The change in x would have to be a little smaller than the change in y

00:13:51.510 --> 00:13:57.319
Since the real estate it gains by moving farther away from 1 is more expensive than the real estate that y

00:13:57.420 --> 00:13:59.420
loses by getting closer to 1

00:13:59.850 --> 00:14:05.020
But from the perspective of the origin point (0, 0) that trade-off is reversed.

00:14:05.020 --> 00:14:07.800
The resulting change to x² is

00:14:07.860 --> 00:14:14.660
smaller than the resulting change to y² since when real estate is measured with respect to (0, 0)

00:14:15.060 --> 00:14:18.710
That move of y towards 1 is the more expensive one

00:14:19.860 --> 00:14:22.620
What this means is that any slight perturbation

00:14:22.800 --> 00:14:28.760
Away from this point where real estate is shared evenly results in an increasing distance from the origin

00:14:29.970 --> 00:14:30.980
The reason we care

00:14:30.980 --> 00:14:33.560
Is that this point is tangent to the inner circle

00:14:33.780 --> 00:14:37.249
So we can also think about it as being a point of the inner circle

00:14:37.249 --> 00:14:44.260
And this will be very useful for higher dimensions. It gives us a reference point to understanding the radius of that inner circle

00:14:45.240 --> 00:14:51.020
Specifically you can ask how much real estate is shared between x and y at this point

00:14:51.020 --> 00:14:54.660
when real estatemeasurements are done with respect to the origin (0, 0)

00:14:55.620 --> 00:14:58.200
For example down here in two dimensions

00:14:58.280 --> 00:15:02.740
both x and y dip below 0.5 in this configuration

00:15:03.020 --> 00:15:10.540
So the total value x²+y² is going to be less than 0.5²+0.5²

00:15:11.550 --> 00:15:17.000
Comparing to this halfway point is really going to come in handy for wrapping our mind around what happens in higher dimensions

00:15:17.640 --> 00:15:20.599
Taking things one step at a time, let's bump it up to three dimensions

00:15:22.500 --> 00:15:26.920
Consider the corner sphere with radius 1 centered at (1, 1, 1).

00:15:26.920 --> 00:15:30.220
The point on that sphere that's closest to the origin

00:15:30.420 --> 00:15:37.360
Corresponds to the configuration of sliders where x, y and z are all reaching down toward 0 and equal to each other

00:15:38.120 --> 00:15:44.040
Again, they all have to go a little beyond that halfway point because the position 0.5

00:15:44.080 --> 00:15:49.680
only accounts for 0.5² (or 0.25) units of real estate

00:15:50.240 --> 00:15:56.020
so with all three coordinates getting 1/3 of a unit of real estate they need to be farther out.

00:15:56.520 --> 00:16:00.772
And again, since this is a point where the corner sphere is tangent to the inner sphere,

00:16:00.780 --> 00:16:02.880
it's also a point of the inner sphere.

00:16:03.200 --> 00:16:06.920
so with reference to the origin (0, 0, 0)

00:16:07.100 --> 00:16:14.000
Think about the amount of real estate shared between x, y, and z in this position corresponding to the tangent point

00:16:14.700 --> 00:16:17.179
It's definitely less than 0.75

00:16:17.760 --> 00:16:21.049
Since all three of these are smaller than 0.5

00:16:21.660 --> 00:16:26.119
so each one has less than 0.25 units of real estate and

00:16:26.700 --> 00:16:30.020
Again, we sit back and feel comfortable with this result, right?

00:16:30.020 --> 00:16:32.479
The inner sphere is smaller than the corner spheres

00:16:33.210 --> 00:16:36.199
But things get interesting when we move up into four dimensions

00:16:37.560 --> 00:16:42.600
Our 2x2x2x2 box is going to have 16 vertices

00:16:42.820 --> 00:16:47.180
at (1, 1, 1, 1), (1, 1, 1, -1) and so on,

00:16:47.180 --> 00:16:51.020
with all possible binary combinations of 1 and -1

00:16:51.899 --> 00:16:56.860
What this means, is that there are 16 units spheres centered at these corners,

00:16:56.860 --> 00:16:58.819
each one tangent to four of its neighbours

00:16:59.670 --> 00:17:02.360
As before, we'll just be focusing on one of them,

00:17:02.360 --> 00:17:04.160
the one centered at (1,1,1,1).

00:17:05.000 --> 00:17:07.080
the point of the sphere closest to the origin

00:17:07.770 --> 00:17:15.050
Corresponds to the configuration of sliders where all four coordinates reach exactly halfway between 1 and 0.

00:17:15.500 --> 00:17:20.140
And that's because when one of the coordinates is 0.5 units away from 1

00:17:20.140 --> 00:17:24.460
it has 0.25 units of real estate with respect to the point 1.

00:17:25.020 --> 00:17:26.610
We do the same trick as before

00:17:26.610 --> 00:17:31.040
Thinking of this now as a point of the inner sphere and measuring things with respect to the origin

00:17:31.500 --> 00:17:35.020
But you can already see what's cool about four dimensions:

00:17:35.020 --> 00:17:38.900
as you switch to thinking of real estate with respect to (0, 0, 0, 0)

00:17:39.420 --> 00:17:44.929
It's still the case that each of these four coordinates has 0.25 units of real estate

00:17:45.420 --> 00:17:48.559
Making for a total of 1 shared between the four coordinates

00:17:49.679 --> 00:17:54.619
In other words that inner sphere is precisely the same size as the corner spheres

00:17:55.230 --> 00:17:57.929
This matches with what you see numerically by the way

00:17:57.929 --> 00:18:04.279
where you can compute the distance between the origin and the corner (1,1,1,1) is the square root of four,

00:18:04.470 --> 00:18:09.439
And then when you subtract off the radius of one of the corners spheres what you get is 1.

00:18:10.020 --> 00:18:16.099
But there's something much more satisfying about seeing it, rather than just computing it in particular

00:18:16.100 --> 00:18:19.429
Here's a cool aspect of the fact that that inner sphere has radius one

00:18:19.860 --> 00:18:23.750
move things around so that all of the real estate goes to the coordinate x and

00:18:23.880 --> 00:18:26.599
You'll end up at the point (1,0,0,0).

00:18:27.150 --> 00:18:31.550
